/* Local Operator TUI — island-dark, borderless, one green.
 *
 * Brand tokens ($lo-*) are injected by OperatorApp.get_css_variables() from
 * local_operator.tui.theme (single source of truth; this file never carries
 * a literal hex). The dark ramp is the product's "island" night.
 *
 * Minimalism contract (density is the brand):
 *   - NO bordered boxes anywhere. Structure comes from symbols, tint, and
 *     spacing. The app draws exactly TWO rules, both structural $lo-dim and
 *     neither of them an edge of anything: the input panel's thin top line
 *     (the focus accent moves to the chevron, never the rule — D23/D24) and
 *     the user prompt's gutter bar, which runs down the left of every row of
 *     a message so a multi-paragraph prompt reads as ONE block. A rule that
 *     closes a shape is a box; a rule that marks a margin is a gutter.
 *   - NO uniform filler rows. Blocks own no outer margin; separation is
 *     ADAPTIVE and rides one opt-in class (.gap-above) that the transcript
 *     container applies only where the rhythm needs it — a run of one-line
 *     notices stays flush, while prose, expanded output and each separate
 *     tool action get a blank row (SPACING_AIRY in widgets/transcript.py).
 *   - one line per tool action while collapsed; each action is a subtle
 *     background-filled card (elevation = a background step, never a border
 *     or shadow) whose FILL carries the outcome
 *   - the accent green means ONE thing: a turn is live. It is spent on exactly
 *     five sites, and the list is exhaustive — `grep -rn 'semantic_color("accent")'
 *     local_operator/tui` plus `$lo-accent` in this file should find no others:
 *       1. the working line's shimmer crest      (shimmer.py)
 *       2. a running tool's glyph                (widgets/tool_card.py)
 *       3. the status band's streaming spinner   (widgets/status_line.py)
 *       4. the focused input chevron             (this file,
 *                                                 #input-dock.-composer-focused)
 *       5. the picker's highlighted command NAME (widgets/command_picker.py)
 *     Everything else is warm neutrals. Links and file paths are the `signal`
 *     blue and deliberately NOT green; `/usage` provider headings are `label`;
 *     the brand glyph in the band is `muted`, because a mark that is always on
 *     screen cannot also mean "running" — see theme.py. Before adding a sixth,
 *     take one away: an accent spent everywhere is an accent spent nowhere.
 *
 * Edge spacing: one cell at the left, right, and bottom screen edges; NONE
 * along the top while scrolling (content meets the top edge). The transcript
 * also ends in one row of ground, so the conversation never touches the
 * composer — see `TranscriptView` for why that row is the transcript's.
 */

Screen {
    background: $lo-bg;
    color: $lo-fg;
    /* Outer inset for the WHOLE app, matching the omp reference: the chrome is
     * borderless, so the breathing room at the terminal's edges is what makes
     * it read as a deliberate surface rather than text that ran out of room.
     * One row top and bottom, one cell left and right. Children then only
     * manage spacing BETWEEN themselves. */
    padding: 1;
    /* A second layer for the toast overlay. Layers are arranged independently
     * over the whole region, which is the property that matters: without one,
     * a toast at the top of the screen would STEAL a row from the transcript
     * and reflow the conversation for five seconds. It stops the LAYOUT
     * reflowing — it does NOT stop the screen being erased, so the host is
     * sized to its card (see `#toast-host`) and not to the width. Everything
     * else stays on `default` (Textual's name for the unset layer), so this
     * declaration changes no existing placement. */
    layers: default toast;
    /* The screen itself NEVER scrolls: the transcript is the scrolling
     * surface and the input panel is docked, so a scrollbar here can only
     * come from a floating overlay. It did. A tall `/usage` card (or any
     * overlay taller than its resting offset allows) pushed the screen's
     * virtual height past its size, which drew a scrollbar the app has no
     * use for AND cost two cells of width — the transcript reflowed behind
     * a popup that is supposed to leave the layout alone. Layers stop the
     * overlay taking a row in the FLOW; only this stops it extending the
     * scrollable region. */
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Transcript takes all the space above the input panel. One cell of edge
 * padding on every side INCLUDING the top: the first line of a turn sitting
 * flush against the terminal's top edge reads as clipped rather than as the
 * start of the conversation. The scrollbar column is reserved permanently
 * (D27) so the right-aligned columns measure stable width. */
TranscriptView {
    height: 1fr;
    /* Right padding is 0 (D21): the permanently reserved scrollbar column
     * below already supplies the right-hand cell, so `1` there made every
     * background-filled tool card one cell wider on the left than the right —
     * visible as an off-centre slab on the one element whose whole job is to
     * look deliberately borderless.
     *
     * The BOTTOM row is the conversation's last row of ground, and it was the
     * one edge of the transcript nobody had spent. Measured at 60x40 with a
     * conversation tall enough to reach the dock: 0 rows between the last
     * transcript row and the composer's fill, so `▌ now do the other one`,
     * `! interrupted` and a failed tool card's tinted slab each sat directly
     * on the panel — the conversation and the input read as one slab.
     *
     * The row belongs to the TRANSCRIPT rather than to `#input-dock`, and that
     * is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
     *   - the aside already reserves this same padding for its card's rows
     *     (`OperatorApp._reserve_ground_for_aside`), so one owner keeps the
     *     flush card-on-composer seam a single number: the reservation
     *     REPLACES this row instead of stacking with it;
     *   - every floating card is placed off the docked panel's `region.y`
     *     (`widgets/overlay.rows_above_dock`), so a padding row on the DOCK
     *     lifts the aside and `/usage` cards a row off the composer. Measured
     *     at 120x40: it put a blank row into the aside's seam, the one join
     *     that was deliberately chosen flush;
     *   - it is the same semantics as the top row above — the conversation's
     *     own first and last row of ground, scrolling with the content rather
     *     than a permanent inset on the frame. It survives `scroll_end`
     *     because a container's padding is inside its scrollable region.
     * The boot layout takes it back (`Screen.boot TranscriptView`), where the
     * column is bottom-aligned and the row could only ever be slack. */
    padding: 1 0 1 1;
    scrollbar-size-vertical: 1;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    scrollbar-background: $lo-bg;
    /* `edge`, not `dim` (D4). The gutter is reserved permanently so the
     * right-aligned columns measure a stable width, which means the THUMB is
     * on screen whenever the transcript overflows by even one row. At `dim`
     * that is a full-cell saturated column running the height of the view —
     * the largest continuous fill in a narrow frame, reading as the right-hand
     * border a borderless design must not have. `edge` keeps it structurally
     * present; hover still lights it to `dim`. */
    scrollbar-color: $lo-edge;
    scrollbar-color-hover: $lo-muted;
}

/* Blocks: zero outer margin, zero padding — no declarations here by design
 * (the minimalism defense asserts their absence; anything uniform here would
 * be the "blank row between everything" regression). */
TranscriptBlock, UserBlock, NoticeBlock, RichBlock, AssistantBlock, ToolCard {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}

/* The ONLY separator in the app: one blank row, applied per block by the
 * transcript container's adaptive spacing rule. */
.gap-above {
    margin-top: 1;
}

/* The prompt's own ink. The gutter bar and the prose are both styled per-span
 * by the widget (see `UserBlock` in widgets/transcript.py, which owns the
 * treatment because it has to wrap the text itself to keep the bar on every
 * row), so this is the base the sheet would fall back to and the one place a
 * reader grepping the stylesheet for "user" finds the pointer. There is
 * deliberately no background here: elevation-as-a-background-step is spent in
 * full on the tool ledger, whose FILL carries the outcome, and a second slab
 * kind on the same surface turns the transcript into a stack of cards. */
UserBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

NoticeBlock {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

RichBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* Assistant markdown renders normal weight in the base ink color. */
AssistantBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* ONE LINE PER ACTION — the row is a background-filled card, one elevation
 * step brighter than the ground (kit surface on bg).
 *
 * The collapsed height is PINNED rather than left to `auto`, and that is
 * load-bearing: a card is built before it is laid out, so its very first
 * row is measured against a guessed width and can be a cell or two wide.
 * Under `auto` that first measurement resolves to two rows and stays there
 * even after the resize rebuilds the row to fit — a ledger of one-line
 * traces silently doubles in height. Pinning makes the worst case a clipped
 * cell for one frame, which is the right failure. */
ToolCard {
    height: 1;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

/* Expanded is the ONE case that is content-sized: the summary row plus one
 * row per line of revealed tool output (each already truncated to width, so
 * nothing reflows). The class beats the type selector above. */
ToolCard.tool-expanded {
    height: auto;
}

/* State reaches the GROUND, not just the glyph: a live action sits one step
 * proud of its settled neighbours, and a failure stops being neutral. Both
 * tints sit near the surface luminance so the ledger never shouts. */
ToolCard.tool-running {
    background: $lo-raised;
}

ToolCard.tool-error {
    background: $lo-tint-danger;
}

/* Mouse affordance: the whole row is the click target for expand/collapse,
 * so the whole row lights on hover. Last rule wins over the state tints —
 * pointing at a row should always look the same. */
ToolCard:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* Keyboard affordance, and the reason the card is a focus stop at all: a row
 * the arrows have landed on has to look landed-on, or Enter is a guess.
 *
 * `tint-select` rather than the hover `overlay` on purpose. They are two
 * different pointers — the mouse is where the hand is, focus is where the
 * KEYBOARD is, and only one of them survives the hand leaving the mouse — so
 * a focused row under a wandering pointer must not lose its mark. The tint is
 * the same cool-green ground the command picker gives its highlighted row,
 * which is already this app's word for "this is the one you are on".
 *
 * Last rule in the block, so it wins over the state tints AND over :hover:
 * where the keyboard is, is the more load-bearing fact. Still no border —
 * elevation is a background step here as everywhere. */
ToolCard:focus {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* The status band is the LAST row of the input panel, below the editor. It reads
 * as a footer for the whole app rather than a caption on the input, and it puts
 * the least volatile information at the screen's least volatile edge.
 *
 * SAME fill as the input panel, not a step down. A second elevation inside one
 * panel read as two stacked bars — a strip of chrome bolted under the input
 * rather than part of it. The panel is one surface; the band is simply its last
 * row, and the blank row above separates them.
 *
 * That blank row is PADDING rather than a vertical margin — the stylesheet
 * permits exactly one such declaration (the adaptive `.gap-above` rule) and a
 * second source of it is how adaptive spacing decays into "a blank row between
 * everything". Because the band and the panel share a fill, a padded row is
 * visually identical to a gap. */
#status-band {
    dock: bottom;
    height: 2;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-dim;
    /* Left padding 0: the dock already insets this panel by one cell, so a
     * symmetric `0 1` stacked a second cell on and put the band's leading glyph
     * at column 2 while the input chevron directly above it, the tool glyphs and
     * the prose all start at column 1. The app has one strong left rail and the
     * bottom row was the only thing missing it. */
    padding: 1 1 0 0;
}

/* Input panel: two widgets for one panel, and the split is load-bearing.
 *
 * `#input-dock` is a POSITIONER — docked to the bottom, full width, no fill of
 * its own. It stays docked in both layouts because a docked child's rows are
 * reserved by the layout engine before anything else is placed: the input is
 * the one widget on screen that may never be clipped or pushed off, and in the
 * flow it would be, on any terminal too short for the splash above it.
 *
 * `#input-shell` is the panel the user sees: the fill, the padding, and — in the
 * boot layout — the clamp that turns it into a centred card. It has to be a
 * separate widget because Textual places a docked child at its edge and its
 * parent's `align-horizontal` cannot move it, so a clamped DOCK would sit
 * against the left edge of the terminal rather than centred. The dock centres
 * this child instead, which in the full-width layout is a no-op.
 *
 * No border of any kind. The elevation step from the transcript's `bg` to this
 * panel's `surface` is the whole separation — a `border-top` rule read as a
 * bottom border on the app, which is exactly the bordered-chrome look the kit
 * exists to avoid. If the panel ever stops reading as separate, the answer is
 * another elevation step, never a line.
 *
 * Padding on all four sides: the editor text used to start flush against the
 * panel's edges, which read as an unfinished box rather than a deliberate
 * borderless field. One cell of breathing room is what makes "no border" look
 * chosen instead of missing. */
#input-dock {
    dock: bottom;
    height: auto;
    align-horizontal: center;
}

#input-shell {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
    padding: 1;
}

#input-row {
    height: auto;
}

#prompt-chevron {
    width: 2;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* Focus moves the accent onto the chevron only — the rule stays dim (D23).
 *
 * Driven by a CLASS the app toggles (see `OperatorApp._sync_composer_focus`),
 * NOT by `#input-dock:focus-within`, which silently never turned back off.
 * Textual re-applies styles on a focus change by walking up from the widget
 * that gained or lost focus until it finds an ancestor whose rules mention
 * `focus-within` (`Screen._update_focus_styles`), and that flag is recorded on
 * the node the rule SELECTS — here the chevron, which is the editor's SIBLING
 * and so is never on that walk. Measured on the shipped build: after the boot
 * `editor.focus()`, blurring the composer left `:focus-within` reporting False
 * while the chevron still painted the accent green on every later frame.
 * A permanently green chevron is not just a missed affordance, it spends the
 * one accent on a composer that is not focused. Class changes go through
 * `App.update_styles`, which re-applies the node AND its descendants, so the
 * chevron follows the class in both directions. */
#input-dock.-composer-focused #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-accent;
}

Editor {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 1;
    max-height: 8;
    border: none;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* TextArea component styling (theme "css" reads these from the widget). */
Editor .text-area--cursor {
    color: $lo-surface;
    background: $lo-fg;
}

Editor .text-area--selection {
    background: $lo-edge;
}

Editor .text-area--placeholder {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

Editor .text-area--gutter {
    color: $lo-dim;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

Editor .text-area--cursor-line {
    background: $lo-surface;
}

Editor .text-area--suggestion {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* ── startup toast ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Top-right transient overlay. The host HUGS its card (`width: auto`) and both
 * are positioned by a Python-set offset and `max-width` (see widgets/toast.py —
 * both depend on the terminal width, which a stylesheet cannot ask for).
 *
 * The host must never be wider than the card. Textual hands a widget its whole
 * region and blanks all of it, so `width: 1fr` here painted the card's 35 cells
 * of notice and erased the remaining 59 cells of transcript on the same rows —
 * the layer keeps the toast out of the layout, not off the screen.
 *
 * Top rather than bottom-right, which is where Textual's own notifications go:
 * the input dock and status band are docked to the bottom, so a bottom toast
 * would cover the editor the user is typing into.
 *
 * The card is BORDERLESS and FILLED — elevation is a background step, never a
 * line (D4). It takes `overlay`, the ramp's top step, because it floats above
 * every other surface; `ToolCard` is the same treatment one step lower. The
 * one cell of horizontal padding is the app's edge-padding unit, so the text
 * does not touch the fill's edge.
 *
 * `height: auto` because the message is one or two lines and the widget
 * decides which — unlike the tool card, this content is measured only once per
 * toast and is never re-flowed mid-life, so the auto-height trap that pins the
 * card does not apply here. */
#toast-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

Toast {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* ---- usage panel (the `/usage` popup) --------------------------------- *
 *
 * Same treatment as the toast, and for the same reasons: it floats above every
 * other surface, so it takes `overlay` (the ramp's top step) as a FILL, with no
 * border — elevation is a background step, never a line (D4).
 *
 * It differs from the toast in what it is FOR, not in how it is placed. It is
 * read rather than glanced at, so it holds focus and is dismissed by a key
 * instead of a timer: nothing about the session changes underneath a table the
 * user is reasoning about.
 *
 * The host HUGS the panel (`width: auto`) and both are positioned by a
 * Python-set offset — the identical rule the toast host carries, for the
 * identical reason. Textual hands a widget its whole region and BLANKS all of
 * it, so a `width: 100%` host on this layer erases every column of every row
 * the panel covers, including the transcript text either side of it. The layer
 * keeps the panel out of the LAYOUT; it does not keep it off the screen. That
 * also rules out `align: center middle` here, which needs a stretched host —
 * the centring is arithmetic in the widget instead, against the measured
 * screen it already has to read to size its own bars. */
#usage-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* `padding: 1 2`, not `0 1`: this card floats OVER the transcript, so its
 * gutter is the only thing separating the report from the text underneath it.
 * One cell was not enough for the panel to read as lifted — the rows touched
 * the fill's edge and the whole card looked cramped. The widget mirrors both
 * numbers in PANEL_PADDING_CELLS/ROWS because it sizes itself (Textual's
 * width/height are border-box), so a change here MUST change those. */
UsagePanel {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* On a terminal too short for the card's normal two-row vertical gutter, the
 * widget spends the decoration rather than cover the docked prompt. The class
 * is set from the SAME `_fit()` result that pins the height, so CSS and Python
 * cannot disagree about whether those rows exist. Horizontal breathing stays. */
UsagePanel.-squeezed {
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* ---- the /btw aside card --------------------------------------------- *
 *
 * Same layer, same host rule, same reason as `#usage-host` above: `width:
 * auto` so the host hugs the card, because a stretched host on an overlay
 * layer blanks every cell of every row it covers — including the transcript
 * either side of the card. Placement is a Python-set offset on the host
 * (`widgets/overlay.anchor_bottom` — bottom-anchored rather than centred,
 * because this card GROWS while it is being read and a centred one would drag
 * the text with it), which is also why there is no `align` here.
 *
 * It differs from the usage card in ONE way that matters to the stylesheet:
 * this card does not take focus. The composer keeps it, because the user is
 * meant to keep typing into the aside, so nothing here may compete for it. */
#aside-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* One cell of horizontal padding is `#input-shell`'s, not the usage card's
 * `1 2`, and that is the point: the card sits directly ON the composer and
 * shares its column, so the same inset puts the card's `▌` spine in the same
 * cell as the composer's `❯`, at every width and in both layouts. (The card's
 * PROSE then sits one cell left of the editor's typed text, because Textual's
 * `TextArea` carries an inherited `padding: 0 1` the composer never overrides
 * — the card lines up with the TRANSCRIPT's user block instead, which is
 * where `^f` forks the question to.)
 *
 * No bottom padding row. Measured at 120x40 with one, the seam between the
 * card and the composer was the widest interval on screen — 2 rows, against 1
 * everywhere else in the stack — because both halves of one unit pushed a
 * padding row into the join that should be tightest, and a reader groups by
 * spacing before fill. The composer's own top padding row is the single row
 * of breathing for both. It also settles a state inconsistency: `-squeezed`
 * already rendered a 1-row seam, so the join silently re-spaced on a drag.
 *
 * The widget mirrors both numbers in PANEL_PADDING_CELLS/ROWS because it sizes
 * itself (Textual is border-box), so a change here MUST change those.
 *
 * `$lo-overlay` over the composer's `$lo-surface` — one elevation step apart,
 * which is how this kit separates stacked surfaces. No border and no rule
 * between them: the step IS the seam, and the two read as one column with the
 * live half at the bottom. */
AsidePanel {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 1 0 1;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* Too short for the vertical gutter: spend the decoration rather than cover
 * the docked prompt. Set from the same `_fit()` result that pins the height. */
AsidePanel.-squeezed {
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* No `pointer: pointer`, and no click targets. Textual 8.2.8 supports the
 * property, so this says NO deliberately rather than by omission. The card
 * owns no input by contract — the ONE composer is pointed at it — so a hit
 * area would be a second input surface competing for focus the aside
 * deliberately leaves in the editor, and `esc`/`^f` are modal keys with no
 * sensible region to click. Clicks are swallowed (`AsidePanel.on_click`); a
 * pointer cursor over a surface that stops every click is a promise the card
 * does not keep. The one mouse gesture that DOES act is the wheel, which
 * walks back through earlier questions. */

/* The conversation recedes while the aside owns the composer. Same rule as
 * the subagent page below — what recedes is what is INERT, and with Enter
 * aimed at the card the transcript is exactly that. It also settles the
 * fragment noise: an 88-cell card over a 120-cell screen leaves ~16 cells of
 * mid-word transcript down each side, interleaving with the card's own rows
 * at full brightness.
 *
 * `opacity`, NOT the `text-opacity` the subagent rule uses: measured, the
 * latter does not reach the mounted transcript blocks (their ink stayed at
 * the full `fg` token) while `opacity` does. 60% rather than the subagent
 * page's 45%, and the difference is the point — an aside is a question ABOUT
 * the conversation, so it has to stay readable behind the card. 60% puts it
 * at 5.92:1 against the card's 11.24:1 answer text; 45% drops it to 3.84:1,
 * too far for content the user may still be reading. */
Screen.aside #transcript {
    opacity: 60%;
}

/* ---- welcome view (new-session empty state) --------------------------- *
 *
 * Lives INSIDE the transcript, so it is handed exactly the region above the
 * input panel without any arithmetic here.
 *
 * `auto`, not `1fr`: in the boot layout below, the splash RESTS ON the input
 * card, and a stretched block cannot rest on anything — it reports the whole
 * region as its height, so the block floats in the middle of it with the card
 * an arbitrary number of rows further down. Content-sized, the widget reports
 * exactly the rows its block occupies (build_welcome_lines returns no padding
 * rows) and the alignment below puts that block against the card. It also
 * settles the /clear case the old `1fr` was chosen for: the "history is
 * untouched" notice mounted under this view now simply follows the block
 * instead of competing with it for the region.
 *
 * The region it is measured against already excludes the input panel's rows,
 * because the panel is DOCKED and the layout engine reserves a docked child's
 * rows before it offers the rest to the flow. That is what keeps the block's
 * height budget honest without any arithmetic here or in the widget.
 *
 * No border, no rule, no fill: the block is the only thing on screen, so the
 * mandate's "structure from symbols, tint and spacing" is all it gets. The
 * accent green is NOT spent here — the logo lockup carries its own hierarchy
 * through the neutral ramp (mark `dim`, wordmark `fg`).
 *
 * HORIZONTAL centring is still computed in the widget, not declared here: what
 * it draws depends on the cells it actually has (the logo tier drops before the
 * hints, which drop before the status rows), so it needs the measured width
 * that `content-align` would hide from it. VERTICAL placement is the
 * stylesheet's alone (`Screen.boot`), so the two can never disagree. */
#welcome {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 0;
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* ── boot layout: the input is a centred card until the session has content ──
 *
 * Everything above assumes the input panel is a full-width band. Before the
 * transcript holds a single block it is not: the panel is a clamped card, and
 * the splash rests directly on it so the two read as ONE column rather than a
 * logo adrift over a bar. The status band stays the card's last row in both
 * layouts, so it travels with the input it belongs to instead of stranding
 * itself at the bottom of the screen, and the pickers keep their place between
 * the input row and the band — inside the clamp, because they are inside the
 * card.
 *
 * `Screen.boot` is the whole switch, and the app flips it in the same call that
 * shows or hides the splash (OperatorApp._set_welcome_visible). Two layouts,
 * ONE condition: a second notion of "the session is empty" is exactly how a
 * centred boot card ends up on screen beside a populated transcript. A second
 * class, `.boot-card`, rides on top of it — not a third layout, a MEASUREMENT:
 * whether this terminal is wide enough for the card to read as one (see the rule
 * below).
 *
 * The clamp is `max(75, 70% of the content box)`, never wider than the content
 * box. It takes three declarations because tcss has no `max()`: `width` is the
 * proportion, `min-width` the floor, `max-width` the degradation — and the
 * degradation BEATS the floor, so at 40 or 20 cells the card is simply as wide
 * as there is room for rather than overflowing a terminal the minimalism suite
 * measures. NOTHING is added to make it read as a card: the panel's existing
 * surface step IS the card (D4), now bounded on both sides instead of running
 * the width of the terminal.
 *
 * `align-vertical: bottom` on the TRANSCRIPT is what puts the splash on the
 * card: the transcript keeps its `1fr` height, so the region it offers the
 * splash is still the engine's own "everything the docked panel did not take",
 * and the content-sized block inside it is pushed to the bottom of that region —
 * which is the card's top edge. Making the transcript content-sized instead
 * looks equivalent and is not: its measured height then depends on the block it
 * contains while the block's budget depends on the region, and the pair settles
 * one row past the bottom of the screen, where a scrollbar appears and reflows
 * the width of everything.
 *
 * WHERE the pair sits is not this rule's, though — it only says the splash and the
 * card touch. The pair is the composition, and a composition resting on the bottom
 * of a 48-row terminal left the upper two thirds of the screen empty, so the app
 * reserves rows BELOW the card as well (OperatorApp._sync_boot_composition) and the
 * two slacks come out even. Reserving them is conditional on their being spare:
 * every row the composition takes is a row the splash's budget loses, and the
 * splash pays in whole sections — at 28 rows the block wants all 20 it is offered,
 * and buying air there would spend the mark, the one thing that screen exists to
 * show. So a short terminal keeps exactly the old behaviour: the pair on the
 * bottom, all the slack above, where nothing needs it.
 *
 * The panel stays DOCKED here. In the flow it would be the last thing placed
 * and the first thing clipped, and on a terminal too short for the splash above
 * it, the input — the one widget that may never disappear — is what would walk
 * off the bottom edge.
 *
 * No `transition:` on purpose. The swap is instant: a half-drawn interpolation
 * of a docked panel reads as a glitch in a terminal, not as motion. */
Screen.boot TranscriptView {
    align-vertical: bottom;
    /* The one row the boot layout does not need — and the LEFT cell restated,
     * because Textual resolves `padding` as one value per rule: a lone
     * `padding-top` here would win the whole declaration and take the spine's
     * left cell with it, moving every row in the region one column out of line
     * with the input chevron below.
     *
     * In the conversation layout the top row keeps a turn's first line off the
     * terminal's top edge, where it would read as clipped. Here the column is
     * bottom-aligned against the card, so that row can only ever be slack — and
     * the screen's own one-cell inset is still above it, so nothing touches the
     * edge either way. It is not free slack, though: under the shared-region
     * budget (WelcomeView.get_content_height) every row this region spends is a
     * row the splash's degradation ladder has to pay for, and the ladder pays in
     * whole sections. Reclaiming a row that buys nothing is what lets the mark
     * survive a `/clear` receipt AND two failed MCP servers at 96x28. Frames with
     * slack are unchanged: the block is bottom-aligned, so a taller region only
     * grows the empty space above. */
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

/* The clamp lives on `.boot-card`, not on `Screen.boot`, because a clamp with no
 * lower guard is worse than no clamp. `max(75, 70%)` insets the panel by 1 to 3
 * cells between 78 and 84 columns — at 80, the commonest terminal, 2 cells on the
 * left and 3 on the right — and an inset smaller than the app's own one-cell
 * gutter does not read as a card. It reads as a full-width bar that is off
 * centre, which is the one thing a borderless surface must never look like:
 * there is no edge to attribute the offset to.
 *
 * So the app measures the card once per resize and adds this class only when the
 * inset is at least 4 cells a side (OperatorApp.on_resize; computing a width in
 * Python is the same move Toast._refit already makes, because a stylesheet cannot
 * ask how wide the terminal is). Below that threshold the panel stays the
 * full-width docked bar the base rule already gives it — the same honest
 * degradation 40 and 20 cells get, arrived at 40 columns earlier.
 *
 * `max-width` is the CAP, in cells, not the old `100%`: unbounded, `70%` resolves
 * to 131 cells on a 190-column terminal and the panel is a bar again — a
 * borderless fill reads as a card only while the ground beside it is legible as
 * margin, and past a hundred cells the fill IS the frame. The `100%` guard it
 * replaces is not lost: the class is only applied where the resolved width leaves
 * at least 8 cells of box, and the geometry suite measures every rendered row
 * against the terminal at 16 and 20 cells, where the floor would overflow. */
Screen.boot.boot-card #input-shell {
    width: 70%;
    min-width: 75;
    max-width: 100;
}

/* ── slash-command picker ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The suggestion list under the editor, inside the input dock and above the
 * bottom-docked status band. Borderless like everything else (D4): the list
 * is held together by the 2-cell selection gutter — which lines up with
 * `#prompt-chevron`, so a highlighted `❯` sits directly beneath the prompt's
 * own `❯` and command names start in the editor's text column. Hence NO left
 * padding here; padding would break that alignment, and a vertical margin is
 * not an option the sheet has (the adaptive `.gap-above` rule is the only
 * vertical margin in the file, deliberately).
 *
 * `height` is written by the widget on every repaint (one row per visible
 * suggestion, plus the overflow count when the list is longer). It is NOT
 * `auto`: a list measured before layout knows its width resolves one row too
 * tall per item and stays there — the same trap `ToolCard` pins itself
 * against. Row backgrounds (selection and hover elevation) are painted into
 * the rich text, since one Static draws every row.
 *
 * The accent lives on the selected command NAME only (D23); this rule paints
 * the ground the unselected rows sit on, which is the dock's own surface so
 * the list reads as part of the input rather than as a popup over it. */
CommandPicker {
    width: 1fr;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* The tool-approval prompt. Same borderless, filled-card language as the tool
 * ledger, because it IS a row in that ledger's story — the difference is that
 * this one is waiting on the user.
 *
 * Height is content-sized rather than pinned (the exception the ToolCard note
 * above explains): a pending prompt is deliberately two rows, the question and
 * the key hints, and it collapses to one when answered. Both counts come from
 * the widget's own state, not from a width-dependent measurement, so the
 * doubling trap that forced ToolCard's pin cannot happen here.
 *
 * Pending sits one elevation step proud — the same "this is live" signal a
 * running tool row uses — and the amber lives in the TEXT (glyph and keys), so
 * the ground never shouts a colour the palette reserves for warnings. */
ApprovalBlock {
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

ApprovalBlock.approval-pending {
    background: $lo-raised;
}

/* Focused is the state that matters here: the prompt takes focus so its answer
 * keys reach it instead of the composer, and the user must be able to SEE where
 * those keys are going.
 *
 * `tint-select` rather than an elevation step, matching `ToolCard:focus`: it is
 * the app's existing word for "this is the one you are on", and it stays
 * distinguishable from hover (`$lo-overlay`) when both are live on the same row.
 * Two focusable transcript blocks must not spell focus two different ways. */
ApprovalBlock:focus {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* ── dock band (subagent task list + todo list) ────────────────────────── *
 *
 * Sits between the transcript and the composer: a transparent POSITIONER that
 * reserves rows only when a panel inside it is visible, so an idle session
 * costs the layout nothing. The two panels each manage their own `display`
 * (hidden when their ledger/store is empty), and each holds ONE filled body
 * (`$lo-surface`, the tool card's elevation) on the ground — the same
 * "elevation is a background step, never a line" rule as everything else. Each
 * slot owns the row of ground BELOW itself (see `.band-slot`), and it is
 * padding on the slot, never a margin, so the element that owns the vertical
 * rhythm is the container. */
/* The band is a normal-flow child INSIDE the docked input-dock (D-15-01).
 * `width: auto` (content-sized) so it does NOT consume the dock's full width
 * and defeat the shell's centering below it — a full-width sibling was the
 * reason the boot card sat flush-left. The band's own slots stretch to the
 * input's width via `.band-slot { width: 1fr }`, which resolves against the
 * band's parent width when the band is content-sized. Zero rows when both
 * panels are hidden; the height of the todo/subagent rows otherwise (D-01). */
#band {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* One row of ground UNDER each slot, not over it, and the direction is the
 * whole point: the transcript now ends in a row of its own ground
 * (`TranscriptView`'s bottom padding), so a top padding row here stacked with
 * it — measured at 120x40 with a todo list up, 2 blank rows between the
 * conversation's last row and the band's slab against 1 everywhere else in the
 * stack, which is the widest interval on screen and reads as the band having
 * come loose. Downward, every join between the column's own STACKED children is
 * one row from exactly one owner: the transcript's trailing row above the band,
 * this row between two visible panels, and this row again above the composer —
 * which used to be no row at all, two same-fill slabs touching and reading as
 * one panel with a todo list inside it.
 *
 * A FLOATING card is the deliberate exception, and the flip made it consistent
 * rather than breaking it. The aside rests on whatever the dock puts at its top
 * (`overlay.stack_on_dock`, gap 0), so with a band up the card's footer now
 * touches the band's slab — measured at 120x40, card footer at row 29 and
 * `Todos · 1/2` at 30. That is the same flush seam the card has on the composer
 * when no band is up, and it is legible for the same reason: the card is
 * `$lo-overlay` over the band's `$lo-surface`, one elevation step, which is
 * this kit's separator. Before the flip the card had a stray ground row under
 * it in this state only, so the one card looked seated on the composer and
 * loose above the band. */
.band-slot {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

.band-body {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
    padding: 0 1;
}

#subagent-header {
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* The rows container must SIZE TO ITS CONTENT, never stretch to fill the
 * band. A Vertical defaults to ``1fr``, which absorbs every free row: with
 * even one subagent the band ballooned to the whole screen and crushed the
 * transcript to a single row (D-01). ``height: auto`` ("as tall as my
 * children") keeps the band as tall as its rows — typically one — so the
 * transcript keeps the space above it. Rows themselves are already ``1``
 * tall; the container is what was overeager. */
#subagent-rows {
    height: auto;
}

/* A row is a live surface: hover lights it, and a running row's trailing cell
 * carries the spinner glyph. Rows are Static; the whole row is the click/Enter
 * target for the full-page subagent view, so hover and focus must read as
 * reachable. */
SubagentRow {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1;
    /* The whole row opens the page, so the pointer says so before the click
     * does — the same affordance the page's own hints carry. Textual emits
     * this as OSC 22; a terminal that ignores it loses nothing. */
    pointer: pointer;
}

SubagentRow:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The row the full-page view is currently showing. The panel stays at full
 * ink in that mode because it is the one thing in the dock that still does
 * something — a row retargets the page — and an interactive list of identical
 * bullets cannot say which one you are reading. `tint-select` is the app's
 * existing word for "this row is the one" (a focused approval carries the
 * same step) and stays distinguishable from the hover tint above. */
Screen.subagent SubagentRow.current {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* ── full-page subagent view ───────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * Opened from a band row (click or Enter). NOT an overlay: it takes the
 * transcript's region of the live screen and leaves the dock exactly where it
 * was, because the reader has to be able to see this is the same app looking
 * somewhere else. Its predecessor was a centred card on the sunken ground,
 * which blacked out the conversation, clipped the child's prose at the card's
 * edge, and had nowhere to put the way back out.
 *
 * Four rows: title, rule, the child's transcript (`1fr`), the hint. The
 * gutter lives HERE rather than on the body, so all four share one left edge;
 * the body's own TranscriptView padding is dropped for the same reason. The
 * right cell is spent so the chrome stops where the body's reserved scrollbar
 * column starts.
 *
 * The BOTTOM cell is this page's share of the transcript's trailing row: the
 * page takes the transcript's region, so it inherits the region's contract that
 * whatever is above the composer ends in a row of ground. Measured at 120x40
 * without it, `↑↓scroll · esc back to conversation · read-only` sat directly on
 * `❯ Read-only — press esc to reply` and the two chrome lines fused into one
 * four-row panel. It cannot come from the body's `TranscriptView` padding,
 * which the class below deliberately zeroes — the hint row is BELOW the body,
 * so the row has to be the page's. */
.subagent-view {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 1 1 1;
}

.subagent-view-title {
    height: 1;
}

.subagent-view-rule {
    height: 1;
}

/* One row of air under the rule. Without it the rule sits directly on the
 * first block, and when that block is a tool card — i.e. whenever the reader
 * has scrolled — a faint full-width line resting on a tinted slab reads as
 * that slab's TOP BORDER, which is the one thing the minimalism contract
 * forbids. The left gutter stays zero: it belongs to the container, so all
 * four rows share one edge. */
.subagent-view-body {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* One quiet row above the hint. Without it the footer sits flush against the
 * last transcript block and reads as one more row of the child's output
 * rather than as the page's own chrome — the same reason the usage card keeps
 * ground above its meta. */
.subagent-view-hints {
    height: 2;
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* Each hint is its own widget so it can be hovered and clicked. Content-sized,
 * because the row is laid out by shedding whole hints rather than by
 * distributing space. The background step is the only part of the hover the
 * stylesheet can own: the hint's ink is chosen per span in rich `Text`, so a
 * `color` rule here would lose to the style the widget builds — the widget
 * repaints its own tones on Enter/Leave, the way a tool card already does.
 * `.actionable` gates it: `read-only` is a state, and a highlight that leads
 * nowhere is worse than none. */
.subagent-view-hint {
    height: 1;
    width: auto;
}

/* The hand pointer, which Textual 8.2.8 DOES support: `styles.pointer` is a
 * real property (`textual.css.constants.VALID_POINTER`), `Screen` walks it on
 * every mouse event, and `App._set_pointer_shape` emits it as OSC 22 — which
 * ghostty implements. The old scope note in docs/VERIFICATION.md searched for
 * `set_mouse_cursor` and a `cursor:` property, neither of which is the name,
 * and concluded the API did not exist. */
.subagent-view-hint.actionable {
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* `$lo-overlay`, the SAME hover ground as `ToolCard:hover` and
 * `SubagentRow:hover` — "pointing at a row should always look the same". On
 * `surface` the step was 1.09:1 against the page ground, roughly a quarter of
 * the app's established hover delta, so the slab was invisible and the whole
 * observable hover was the ink lift. */
.subagent-view-hint.actionable:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The dock in subagent mode: PRESENT and RECEDED, never removed.
 *
 * `text-opacity` and not `color`, because every one of these paints rich
 * `Text` with its ink already chosen — a `color` declaration would be
 * overridden by the styles the widget builds, and the greying would silently
 * do nothing. Opacity blends whatever was painted toward the ground, which is
 * exactly "this belongs to the session you are not currently reading".
 *
 * 45%: enough that the composer reads as unavailable at a glance, not so much
 * that the status band stops being legible — it is still the parent's live
 * state, and a subagent page is precisely where someone watches for the
 * parent finishing.
 *
 * `#subagent-panel` is deliberately NOT in this list. It is the one thing in
 * the dock that still does something in this mode (a row retargets the page),
 * and the mode's whole visual argument is that what recedes is what is inert. */
Screen.subagent #prompt-chevron,
Screen.subagent #todo-panel {
    text-opacity: 45%;
}

/* The status band is NOT greyed, and the composer is not greyed either.
 *
 * The band carries the parent's live state, including the streaming spinner —
 * accent site 3 of the five. At 45% that green blends to roughly 2.6:1 and the
 * shimmer loses half its amplitude, so the mode would put out the one mark
 * answering the question a reader on this page is most likely waiting on
 * ("has the parent finished?"). The rule the mode paints by is that what
 * recedes is what is INERT; the band is not inert, it is just not yours.
 *
 * The composer keeps its ink because in this mode its placeholder is a
 * SENTENCE about the mode (`Read-only — press esc to reply`), not an
 * invitation, and a statement nobody can read is not a statement. Its
 * chevron carries the greying instead: a dead spine marker over a live one is
 * the whole signal, at no cost to legibility. */

/* The chevron's focus accent is OFF in this mode, stated rather than inferred
 * from where focus happens to be. The accent means "a turn is live" (rule 4 of
 * the five sites in the preamble) and must never sit on a field that refuses
 * input — that is a property of the MODE, not of the focus chain, so the mode
 * says it. Higher specificity by one TYPE than the focus rule above, so it
 * wins outright whatever the dock's class says. (It also used to be the only
 * thing holding: the focus accent was driven by `#input-dock:focus-within`,
 * which never turned back off — see that rule for the mechanism.) */
Screen.subagent #input-dock #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-dim;
}


/* ── /resume session picker ────────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * A floating read surface (overlay ramp, borderless, content-sized, Esc to
 * dismiss) because it answers a "read a list, pick a row" question. It differs
 * from the other read surfaces in being two-way:
 * it owns a cursor and hands an id back to the caller.
 *
 * `max-width` rather than a fixed width so an 80-column terminal gets the
 * card it can hold; the widget measures its own columns against PICKER_WIDTH
 * and truncates the name, which is the one column that can give up cells. */
SessionPickerScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: $lo-sunken;
}

SessionPickerScreen .session-picker {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 80;
    max-height: 80%;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#session-picker-body {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-fg;
}
